-  "viz.render": "Render a real, data-bound visualization spec from inline rows (or a datasetRef). Three input modes: supply chartType+encodings for explicit mode; omit chartType for recommender-driven suggest mode; OR supply a structured `intent` {goal, measures[], dimensions[], chartFamily?, measureRef?} (instead of chartType/encodings \u2014 the deterministic half of NL\u2192viz) where the named fields drive encoding, the goal+data drive the recommender pick, an optional chartFamily post-filters it, and an optional governed measureRef lights the measure narrative under output.includeA11y. Returns spec for the five tabular Vega-Lite families (additional ECharts output opt-in). The eight ECharts-primary families return echartsSpec with spec omitted and output.echarts:true plus reason:echarts-primary-family, even if the caller passed false with a synthesized a11y description; compact by default with a specRef trio for pipeline reuse. Supports 13 chart types: 5 tabular (bar, line, area, scatter, heatmap) in suggest/explicit/intent mode, plus 8 explicit-only (treemap, sunburst, sankey, force_graph, chord, choropleth, bubble_map, flow_map). On the CARTESIAN color channel you may supply an explicit `encodings.color.range` \u2014 an array of 2+ hex colors (e.g. [\"#1F6FEB\",\"#D1242F\"]) applied in order to the distinct series, overriding the baked OODS categorical palette (e.g. a 2-color presence scale). Cartesian nominal/ordinal color only: a range on a non-color channel is rejected, on an ECharts-primary type (treemap/sankey/etc.) fails loud (OODS-V145), and on a continuous color scale is ignored with a warning. certify grades the supplied range exactly as it grades the palette, so a low-chroma or low-contrast 'absent' color truthfully fails the contrast pillar \u2014 prefer chromatic colors above the chroma floor. For the categorical ECharts-primary types (treemap/sunburst/sankey/force_graph/chord), more distinct color groups than the 6-slot OODS palette recycles a color across two groups and is surfaced as a never-cycle warning (OODS-V146). For chord and force_graph link integrity, a link naming a node that does not exist fails loud (OODS-V147), and a duplicate directed (source,target) link \u2014 chord/force_graph are directed, so A\u2192B and B\u2192A are distinct \u2014 is surfaced as a warning (OODS-V148).\n\nSCOPE. theme (light|dark, default light) and brand (A|B, default A) resolve the generated CSS token scopes for chart pixels. Omission equals explicit light/A. Scope changes chart content and SVG hashes. The flat token export is unchanged. HC token scopes remain exported, but HC pixels are deferred because Canvas/CanvasText require the user agent. Series retain the light palette where tokens declare no theme-specific palette.",
-  "dashboard.render": "Render a composed metric-overview dashboard (KPI row + trend + breakdown + optional geo map) from a declarative DashboardSpec of shared datasets + panels. Composes each panel through the in-process viz engine with deterministic auto-layout, headless KPI compute, and cross-filter linking. Returns the resolved layout, per-panel compiled specs (Vega-Lite or ECharts, geo registration preserved), KPI values, a dashboard-level a11y block, and one specRef for pipeline reuse.\n\nPANEL IDENTITY. Every successful chart panel returns the contentHash produced by its in-process viz.render call; it therefore matches a standalone viz.render call with the identical spec and data. Set the call-level output.includeNormalizedSpec flag to also return each successful chart panel's NormalizedVizSpec. That IR can be passed to artifact.certify as {spec} alone for a cartesian chart, or as {spec,data} with exactly the matching hierarchy, sankey, chord, network, or geo branch for an ECharts-primary chart. These per-panel identity fields are attached after the dashboard's canonical {panels,layout} projection is cached and hashed, so they do not feed the dashboard contentHash.\n\nHTML IDENTITY. When output.html=true, outputHtmlHash is SHA-256 over the exact returned HTML bytes. It is evidence of deterministic output for that call and runtime, not part of the ECharts certified-matrix/renderHash epoch. All 11 admitted chart panel types draw server-rendered SVG; placeholders are reserved for error panels. Chord and flow_map stay excluded from dashboards (#881). output.contrastScan is a four-pair brand-token preflight, not whole-document WCAG certification.\n\nBRAND. Pass brand:'A'|'B' to select the palette used by both the tokens inlined into output.html and the four-pair contrast preflight; omit it for the previous behavior. Per-panel contentHash and dashboard contentHash are brand-invariant because brand applies at HTML/SVG emission time, while outputHtmlHash is brand-variant.\n\nSCOPE. theme (light|dark, default light) and brand (A|B, default A) resolve the generated CSS token scopes for chart pixels. Omission equals explicit light/A. Scope changes chart content and SVG hashes. The flat token export is unchanged. HC token scopes remain exported, but HC pixels are deferred because Canvas/CanvasText require the user agent. Series retain the light palette where tokens declare no theme-specific palette. HTML sets data-theme and data-brand on the document. Governed measureRef resolution defaults to true: KPI refs bind field/aggregate, chart refs decorate narrative only. Unknown refs produce OODS-V130; missing resolved fields produce OODS-V137 through the panel error policy. Explicit resolveMeasures:false opts out of ref resolution; inputs without refs retain their rendered bytes.",
-  "artifact.certify": "Certify a Forge NormalizedVizSpec IR (the same IR viz.render emits as normalizedSpec), optionally with the chart's DATA. Returns a per-pillar verdict summary (pillars:{a11yEquivalence,determinism,contrast,accuracy} \u2014 five-valued on contrast, four-valued on accuracy) that disaggregates what was actually checked, plus \u2014 on the cartesian path \u2014 a folded conformance gate. Read-only: it reads the IR and the operand, never rebuilds or re-recommends.\n\nTWO COVERAGE CLASSES. The 5 cartesian types (bar/line/area/scatter/heatmap) return coverage:'certified' with a conformant boolean. conformant folds the graded pillars: 'fail' OR 'ungradeable' on contrast or accuracy pulls it false \u2014 ungradeable (s175) means grading was ATTEMPTED and failed for a reason outside the spec (an unresolvable canvas token, an evaluator fault); 'unchecked' (nothing to grade: no colour-bearing unit, no operand) and 'exempt' leave it a11y-driven. Both enums are five-/four-valued now (pass/fail/ungradeable/unchecked[/exempt]); an exhaustive consumer switch must handle 'ungradeable'. The 8 ECharts-primary types (treemap/sunburst/sankey/force_graph/choropleth/bubble_map/flow_map/chord) return coverage:'uncertified' / conformant:null \u2014 a DISTINCT verdict, not a failure, and NOT a statement that nothing was checked. Read the pillars, not the coverage value.\n\nTHE OPTIONAL `data` OPERAND (s172). An ECharts-primary IR is metadata-only by design (data:{values:[]}, encoding:{}) \u2014 the chart's nodes, links, rows and geometry live in viz.render's data branch and never enter the IR. Pass that SAME branch as `data` (exactly one of hierarchy | sankey | chord | network | geo, matching the mark trait) and certify lights two more pillars for these types AND runs the a11y-equivalence rules warn-first over it. Omit it and those two pillars stay 'unchecked' with a note naming the missing operand, and no a11y rule runs. `data` on a CARTESIAN spec is a structured error (OODS-V123): those certify from the IR alone, so it would be dead input.\n\nDETERMINISM. Cartesian: two independent Vega-Lite compiles, byte-compared, hashed \u2014 PLUS two independent @oods/viz-render renders byte-compared for every cartesian chart, including contrast-exempt charts (the first reported as the OPTIONAL determinism.renderHash; a nondeterministic render fails `stable` and pulls conformant false \u2014 the pillar is falsifiable, not a compile tautology). ECharts-primary WITH `data`: contentHash identifies the retained projected option from the first of two independent adapter emissions and still equals viz.render's for the same (spec, data) pair. renderHash identifies the first normalized SVG whenever server-side rendering succeeds; the second independently emitted option is rendered as the proof, and `stable` requires both option and render equality. A typed first-render fault keeps contentHash, omits renderHash, and makes stable false; bubble_map without inline geometry keeps the option proof and names the no-server-map limitation. The certified runtime contract and render-hash epoch live at packages/viz-render/certified-matrix.json. Scope, stated: certify claims nothing about what the CALLER rendered (the render leg is certify's own deterministic render); the render never feeds contentHash; and cross-process renderHash identity is bounded by the locally certified runtime axes in that record.\n\nACCURACY. Cartesian: four declared reader-only structural rules over the IR + the compiled spec \u2014 non-zero bar baseline (OODS-V150), dual axis (V151), area-encodes-linear (V152), aggregation-hiding (V153). ECharts-primary WITH `data`: a per-type set over the operand \u2014 treemap/sunburst V154 (a node value area or angle cannot encode) and V155 (an explicit parent that is not the sum of its children); sankey V156 (negative or non-finite link value), V157 (a node height that is not the flow its links carry) and V158 (a duplicate directed flow); chord V156; choropleth V159 (a region matched to rows with CONFLICTING joined values). force_graph, bubble_map and flow_map offer NOTHING \u2014 a stated position, not a gap, and s173 m01 splits it because the two halves differ: force_graph's distortion candidates are adapter constants, while bubble_map's colorScale and flow_map's strengthField ARE authorable through the branch and simply have no rule written for them yet (a scope decision on the record, not an impossibility). No scorer, no corpus, no render step; the rules read, so the contentHash is unmoved by them.\n\nREAD accuracy:'pass' PRECISELY: none of the rules OFFERED for that chart type positively detected its distortion \u2014 not that the chart is accurate. accuracySummary.rulesEvaluated says how many actually resolved their operand. On the ECharts path 'pass' additionally requires rulesEvaluated > 0, so zero resolved rules reports 'unchecked' with a note saying whether the offered set was empty or a precondition was absent. findings[] therefore carries THREE families told apart by code: OODS-A11Y-<rule.id>, OODS-V150..V153, OODS-V154..V159. On the CARTESIAN path a firing rule pulls conformant false; on the uncertified path conformant STAYS null (no folded claim is made there), so read pillars.accuracy and findings[].\n\nA11Y-EQUIVALENCE runs WARN-FIRST for these 8. Supply `data` and the 16-rule equivalence engine evaluates over the operand-built table and narrative, each rule reporting pass, fail, or NOT-APPLICABLE with its absent precondition named \u2014 so an absent precondition is no longer indistinguishable from a meaningful pass. Failures reach findings[] as OODS-A11Y-<rule.id> at their NATIVE severity (an error-severity rule reports 'error'; nothing is remapped and nothing blocks). Not-applicable rules reach the top-level a11yNotApplicable[] channel as {rule, preconditionAbsent} in rule order \u2014 present (as [] when no rule was not-applicable) exactly when the engine ran on this data-backed path, and ABSENT on a {spec}-only call, on the cartesian path, and when the engine faulted (the partial list is discarded and notes[] says so). A not-applicable rule never enters findings[]. pillars.a11yEquivalence stays 'unchecked' on this path DELIBERATELY: warn-first enumerates what enforcement will require without moving a verdict, and the flip to pass/fail is a future enforce step. Without the operand there is nothing to evaluate and no a11y findings appear. The accessible table and narrative are generated on the data-backed path.\n\nCONTRAST is RENDER-MEASURED for the 5 cartesian types: certify renders the compiled spec through @oods/viz-render and grades the SERIES-TO-PAINT ASSIGNMENT the data marks actually carry \u2014 duplicates retained, so a recycled palette (more consumed series than baked hexes) truthfully fails as a \u0394E00=0 pair \u2014 against the requested CSS scope canvas: role-C mark-vs-background WCAG 3:1 (normative) + role-A categorical CIEDE2000 distinguishability (min-over-CVD), config.tokens overrides honoured. Unit classification still reads the compiled bytes, so a chart that baked no OODS palette can never certify contrast:'pass', and non-palette paints are author chrome \u2014 skipped, never failed. ECharts contrast is render-measured when `data` is supplied: visible chart fills and strokes feed Role C, the semantic N-long category assignment feeds Role A with genuine recycling duplicates retained, and the two grades combine by worst verdict. The 5 categorical families are graded; the 3 geo families remain exempt while still retaining normalized render evidence. spec-only calls retain the reconstructed baked-palette verdict and its baked-bytes caveat. An ordinal-categorical bubble_map colour is still not graded \u2014 as of s172 that rests on the exempt-all-geo ruling alone, since `data` exposes the colorField and the colorScale it renders on (s173 m01 corrects s172's wording: the RANGE is reachable on no path \u2014 the branch has no range field, so an ordinal bubble_map paints from Forge's own categorical list). contrastResults retains the requested theme/brand, verdict and measurement evidence; exempt and unchecked results do not claim a canvas ratio.\n\nTHEME AND BRAND (s190): theme light|dark and brand A|B select the same scoped CSS colors as viz.render; defaults are light/A. data-backed ECharts paints come from the rendered projected option; geo render evidence remains exempt with no canvas ratio. HC token exports exist, but HC pixels are deferred. accuracyRules names the offered rule codes; accuracySummary.rulesEvaluated still counts only resolved operands.\n\nIF YOUR CLIENT CACHES THIS TEXT (s173 m01): tool EXECUTION is read fresh from the built server on every call, so a Forge update reaches your results immediately \u2014 but this description and the input schema are what your connector advertised AT CONNECT TIME, so after a Forge release you are reading the old contract until you RECONNECT. When a Forge changelog says an input or a verdict moved, reconnect before trusting the schema you already have.",
+  "viz.render": "Render a real, data-bound visualization spec from inline rows (or a datasetRef). Four input modes: supply pattern with an exact catalog id (for example pattern:viz:simple-bar) to render its bundled rows, encodings and presentation; supply chartType+encodings for explicit mode; omit chartType for recommender-driven suggest mode; OR supply a structured `intent` {goal, measures[], dimensions[], chartFamily?, measureRef?} (instead of chartType/encodings \u2014 the deterministic half of NL\u2192viz) where the named fields drive encoding, the goal+data drive the recommender pick, an optional chartFamily post-filters it, and an optional governed measureRef lights the measure narrative under output.includeA11y. Returns spec for the five tabular Vega-Lite families (additional ECharts output opt-in). The eight ECharts-primary families return echartsSpec with spec omitted and output.echarts:true plus reason:echarts-primary-family, even if the caller passed false with a synthesized a11y description; compact by default with a specRef trio for pipeline reuse. Supports 13 chart types: 5 tabular (bar, line, area, scatter, heatmap) in suggest/explicit/intent mode, plus 8 explicit-only (treemap, sunburst, sankey, force_graph, chord, choropleth, bubble_map, flow_map). On the CARTESIAN color channel you may supply an explicit `encodings.color.range` \u2014 an array of 2+ hex colors (e.g. [\"#1F6FEB\",\"#D1242F\"]) applied in order to the distinct series, overriding the baked OODS categorical palette (e.g. a 2-color presence scale). Cartesian nominal/ordinal color only: a range on a non-color channel is rejected, on an ECharts-primary type (treemap/sankey/etc.) fails loud (OODS-V145), and on a continuous color scale is ignored with a warning. certify grades the supplied range exactly as it grades the palette, so a low-chroma or low-contrast 'absent' color truthfully fails the contrast pillar \u2014 prefer chromatic colors above the chroma floor. For the categorical ECharts-primary types (treemap/sunburst/sankey/force_graph/chord), more distinct color groups than the 6-slot OODS palette recycles a color across two groups and is surfaced as a never-cycle warning (OODS-V146). For chord and force_graph link integrity, a link naming a node that does not exist fails loud (OODS-V147), and a duplicate directed (source,target) link \u2014 chord/force_graph are directed, so A\u2192B and B\u2192A are distinct \u2014 is surfaced as a warning (OODS-V148).\n\nSCOPE. theme (light|dark|hc, default light) and brand (A|B, default A) resolve the generated CSS token scopes for chart pixels. Omission equals explicit light/A. Scope changes chart content and SVG hashes. HC emits the scope declarations verbatim, including Canvas/CanvasText; computed paints are verified under forced-colors browser emulation. Bar, line, area and scatter have measured HC SVGs. Heatmap and the eight ECharts-primary types return OODS-V165 for HC SVG requests because their measured renderers substitute undeclared paints. Specs remain available without SVG. No server-side system-color hex palette is invented. Series retain the light palette where tokens declare no theme-specific palette. PATTERNS. All 21 source identities are selectable; patterns requiring unsupported interaction or structural layout return OODS-V167 with named reasons and remain authoring-only in the pattern registry. Combining pattern with explicit data, encodings, intent or identity/presentation overrides returns OODS-V166. Brand, theme, strictFields and output controls remain available; authored source data and presentation are bundled for portable rendering.",
+  "dashboard.render": "Render a composed metric-overview dashboard (KPI row + trend + breakdown + optional geo map) from a declarative DashboardSpec of shared datasets + panels. Composes each panel through the in-process viz engine with deterministic auto-layout, headless KPI compute, and cross-filter linking. Returns the resolved layout, per-panel compiled specs (Vega-Lite or ECharts, geo registration preserved), KPI values, a dashboard-level a11y block, and one specRef for pipeline reuse.\n\nPANEL IDENTITY. Every successful chart panel returns the contentHash produced by its in-process viz.render call; it therefore matches a standalone viz.render call with the identical spec and data. Set the call-level output.includeNormalizedSpec flag to also return each successful chart panel's NormalizedVizSpec. That IR can be passed to artifact.certify as {spec} alone for a cartesian chart, or as {spec,data} with exactly the matching hierarchy, sankey, chord, network, or geo branch for an ECharts-primary chart. These per-panel identity fields are attached after the dashboard's canonical {panels,layout} projection is cached and hashed, so they do not feed the dashboard contentHash.\n\nHTML IDENTITY. When output.html=true, outputHtmlHash is SHA-256 over the exact returned HTML bytes. It is evidence of deterministic output for that call and runtime, not part of the ECharts certified-matrix/renderHash epoch. All 11 admitted chart panel types draw server-rendered SVG in light and dark; HC draws the four supported Cartesian types and records unsupported paints as error panels with placeholders. Chord and flow_map stay excluded from dashboards (#881). output.contrastScan is a four-pair brand-token preflight, not whole-document WCAG certification. HC scans return zero numerically graded pairs with a forced-colors explanation.\n\nBRAND. Pass brand:'A'|'B' to select the palette used by both the tokens inlined into output.html and the four-pair contrast preflight; omit it for the previous behavior. Per-panel contentHash and dashboard contentHash are brand-invariant because brand applies at HTML/SVG emission time, while outputHtmlHash is brand-variant.\n\nSCOPE. theme (light|dark|hc, default light) and brand (A|B, default A) resolve the generated CSS token scopes for chart pixels. Omission equals explicit light/A. Scope changes chart content and SVG hashes. HC emits the scope declarations verbatim, including Canvas/CanvasText; computed paints are verified under forced-colors browser emulation. Bar, line, area and scatter have measured HC SVGs. Heatmap and the eight ECharts-primary types return OODS-V165 for HC SVG requests because their measured renderers substitute undeclared paints. Specs remain available without SVG. No server-side system-color hex palette is invented. Series retain the light palette where tokens declare no theme-specific palette. HTML sets data-theme and data-brand on the document. Governed measureRef resolution defaults to true: KPI refs bind field/aggregate, chart refs decorate narrative only. Unknown refs produce OODS-V130; missing resolved fields produce OODS-V137 through the panel error policy. Explicit resolveMeasures:false opts out of ref resolution; inputs without refs retain their rendered bytes.",
+  "artifact.certify": "Certify the Forge NormalizedVizSpec returned by viz.render. Returns coverage, a measured conformant result, findings, and four pillars: a11yEquivalence, determinism, contrast and accuracy. Read-only: it reads the IR and the matching data operand without rebuilding or recommending a chart.\n\nDECLARED COVERAGE. The five Cartesian types certify from the IR alone. The eight ECharts-primary types certify under the declared operand profile when the same data branch used by viz.render is supplied. ECharts conformance requires a11yEquivalence:'pass', contrast neither 'fail' nor 'ungradeable', stable determinism, and accuracy:'pass' with rulesEvaluated > 0 and no detected distortion. A certified chart can be conformant:false; inspect the pillars and findings. No Vega-Lite compile is added to the ECharts profile. Spec-only ECharts calls remain coverage:'uncertified' with conformant:null and notes naming the missing operand. Unmodeled marks remain explicitly uncertified.\n\nDATA. An ECharts-primary IR carries metadata; nodes, links, rows and geometry live in its original branch. Supply exactly the matching hierarchy, sankey, chord, network or geo branch as data. The tool validates it against the mark trait. Data on a Cartesian IR is OODS-V123 because those charts certify from their IR. HTML or invalid IR is OODS-V126.\n\nACCESSIBILITY. The 16 equivalence rules evaluate the operand-built table and narrative. Error-severity failures fail the ECharts pillar and conformance. Warnings stay visible without failing the pillar. Findings keep their native severity. a11yNotApplicable lists rules whose declared preconditions were absent; absence is not a meaningful pass. This channel is present when the ECharts engine completed, including [] if no rule was inapplicable, and absent for spec-only, Cartesian or incomplete engine evaluations. A completed engine with an individual rule fault retains its findings and not-applicable results while failing the pillar. An evaluator fault cannot produce a passing accessibility pillar.\n\nACCURACY. Cartesian rules read the IR and compiled spec: non-zero bar baseline (V150), dual axis (V151), area scaling (V152), undisclosed aggregation (V153). ECharts rules read data: invalid hierarchy values/non-additive parents (V154/V155); invalid sankey/chord link values (V156); inconsistent sankey node throughput/duplicate links (V157/V158); conflicting choropleth join values (V159); negative or non-finite bubble size (OODS-V168), radius-based bubble scaling (OODS-V169), conflicting rows at the same bubble coordinates (OODS-V170), negative or non-finite flow strength (OODS-V171), duplicate directed geographic flows (OODS-V172), and duplicate directed force-graph links (OODS-V173). Reciprocal graph links and reciprocal geographic flows are distinct. Bubble scaling reads the public renderer's default linear size behavior; certification does not repair its pixels. accuracyRules lists offered codes, while accuracySummary counts only rules whose preconditions resolved. Pass means no offered rule detected its distortion, not a general promise of accuracy; zero evaluated ECharts rules is unchecked and cannot yield conformant:true.\n\nDETERMINISM. Two independent compiler/adapter emissions are compared and rendered independently. contentHash identifies the first canonical compiled spec or projected ECharts option; renderHash identifies its normalized SVG and matches viz.render for the same operand/scope. Stable requires both identities to repeat. A first-render fault keeps contentHash, omits renderHash and fails stability. A bubble_map without inline geometry keeps the option proof, omits renderHash and names the no-server-map limitation. Proof is bounded by packages/viz-render/certified-matrix.json and covers certify's own rendering, not a caller's rendering.\n\nCONTRAST. Cartesian charts and ECharts calls with data grade actual rendered paints. Role C checks mark-to-canvas WCAG 3:1; Role A checks categorical CIEDE2000 distinguishability across normal vision and three CVD simulations, retaining recycled-palette duplicates. Missing baked OODS palette bytes cannot produce contrast:'pass'. The three geo families retain the standing contrast exemption with their render evidence; this includes ordinal bubble maps. Spec-only ECharts calls retain the reconstructed palette grade and caveat. Cartesian conformance still fails for fail or ungradeable contrast/accuracy; unchecked and exempt retain their existing semantics. Read every pillar's enum, including ungradeable. contrastResults reports the requested theme/brand and measurement evidence; exemptions do not claim a canvas ratio.\n\nSCOPE. Theme light|dark|hc and brand A|B select the same CSS scope as rendering, default light/A. HC contrast returns exempt, measured:false and reason:forced-colors, with no numeric grade. Accessibility, accuracy and determinism retain their normal evaluation; an actual HC render failure fails determinism and conformance. Four Cartesian types have HC SVG proof; heatmap and the eight ECharts-primary types retain measured undeclared-paint failures. After a release that changes a schema or verdict, reconnect clients that cache tool descriptions and schemas.",
